WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.595
Here's a question we rarely ask.

2
00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:08.561
You split a job across four threads, and the total comes out right.

3
00:00:08.561 --> 00:00:11.936
Does that prove the four of them worked at the same time?

4
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:17.757
At the end of the year, the restaurant's owner wants one number: the total card fees.

5
00:00:17.757 --> 00:00:24.285
Every card payment costs a small fee, there are two million payments, and each fee takes a little arithmetic.

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.992
After closing, four cooks stay, and the owner splits the pile four ways.

7
00:00:28.992 --> 00:00:34.669
Fair, until one pile turns out slower, and three cooks stand around while the fourth works.

8
00:00:34.669 --> 00:00:36.915
Which is how most group projects go.

9
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.472
Splitting once, up front, is a guess.

10
00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:44.528
What we want is every cook busy until the end, with no manager handing out work.

11
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:47.696
Here's how a smarter kitchen does it.

12
00:00:47.696 --> 00:00:49.228
Each cook keeps a stack.

13
00:00:49.228 --> 00:00:51.526
The first cook takes the whole pile.

14
00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:52.037
Too big?

15
00:00:52.037 --> 00:00:56.442
He splits it, puts one half on top of his stack, and keeps the other.

16
00:00:56.442 --> 00:00:57.336
Still too big?

17
00:00:57.336 --> 00:01:01.103
Split again, until a bundle is small enough to just add up.

18
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:03.779
Now an idle cook doesn't wait to be told.

19
00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:07.179
He takes the bundle at the bottom of a busy cook's stack.

20
00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:11.354
That's the oldest one, split highest up, so it's the biggest there is.

21
00:01:11.354 --> 00:01:14.992
He splits it on his own stack, and he's busy for a long time.

22
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.208
Two more steals, and all four cooks are busy.

23
00:01:18.208 --> 00:01:20.013
Nobody handed anything out.

24
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:21.469
Notice the two ends.

25
00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:25.424
A cook works from the top of his own stack, the smallest bundle.

26
00:01:25.424 --> 00:01:28.019
Thieves take from the bottom, the biggest.

27
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:31.231
It's the only kitchen where stealing is good manners.

28
00:01:31.231 --> 00:01:34.455
Just take the big one, and you won't be back for a while.

29
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:35.519
Now the names.

30
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:38.197
The cooks are the workers of a ForkJoinPool.

31
00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:41.666
Each stack is a deque, a line you can use from both ends.

32
00:01:41.666 --> 00:01:46.475
Putting half on your stack is fork, and waiting for that half's answer is join.

33
00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:54.327
The size where splitting stops is the threshold, the small bundles are leaves, and taking from another's bottom is work stealing.

34
00:01:55.233 --> 00:02:02.195
In code, compute splits its range, forks the left half, computes the right half itself, then joins the left.

35
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.563
The lesson's WorkStealingDemo works out the fee on all two million payments.

36
00:02:07.563 --> 00:02:11.300
A threshold of 50,000 gives 64 leaves, on four workers.

37
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.988
It also counts the most leaves running at the same moment: a referee.

38
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.936
The pool finished in 869 milliseconds, against 1,021 for one thread.

39
00:02:21.936 --> 00:02:25.732
64 leaves, only 7 steals, and the same total.

40
00:02:25.732 --> 00:02:31.047
And the referee says 4: all four workers computed side by side.

41
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:38.169
Only 1.2 times faster, to be honest, because other programs kept most of that machine's cores busy.

42
00:02:38.169 --> 00:02:41.307
On a quiet machine, it can get close to four.

43
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:42.834
Now swap two lines.

44
00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:47.352
Fork the left half, join it, and only then compute the right half.

45
00:02:47.352 --> 00:02:48.174
It compiles.

46
00:02:48.174 --> 00:02:51.117
How many leaves run at the same moment now?

47
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:54.152
One.

48
00:02:54.152 --> 00:03:01.084
The total is still right, the leaves still landed on all four workers, and there were more steals than before.

49
00:03:01.084 --> 00:03:03.541
It was a little slower than one thread.

50
00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:05.064
Here's why.

51
00:03:05.064 --> 00:03:10.451
The cook puts half on his stack, then waits for it before the other half even exists.

52
00:03:10.451 --> 00:03:15.331
So the halves run one after the other, at every split, hopping between cooks.

53
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:18.942
It's teamwork the way a relay race is teamwork.

54
00:03:18.942 --> 00:03:21.145
Four runners, one running at a time.

55
00:03:21.145 --> 00:03:25.305
So the right total proves the work got done, not that it was shared.

56
00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.997
Fork one half, compute the other, then join.

57
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.886
You're more likely to meet this pool through one word: parallel.

58
00:03:32.886 --> 00:03:39.652
A parallel stream splits its data the same way, on the common pool, one ForkJoinPool the whole JVM shares.

59
00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:47.632
It's the team of helpers the whole restaurant borrows: parallel streams, and every CompletableFuture step that names no pool.

60
00:03:47.632 --> 00:03:51.909
Here it has three workers, plus the thread that started the stream.

61
00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:55.483
So what happens when a helper picks up the phone?

62
00:03:55.483 --> 00:04:02.798
StarvationDemo runs 16 lookups in a parallel stream, each a 300 millisecond call to the slow stock service.

63
00:04:02.798 --> 00:04:06.421
Meanwhile, someone hands the common pool a tiny task.

64
00:04:06.421 --> 00:04:07.925
How long does it wait?

65
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.083
1,202 milliseconds.

66
00:04:12.083 --> 00:04:20.015
A task that takes microseconds waited over a second, because every helper was on hold with the stock service.

67
00:04:20.233 --> 00:04:22.164
Here the kitchen picture breaks.

68
00:04:22.164 --> 00:04:26.326
A real kitchen would spot a cook on the phone and share out his pile.

69
00:04:26.326 --> 00:04:28.981
The pool can't see that a worker is waiting.

70
00:04:28.981 --> 00:04:30.308
It just has one fewer.

71
00:04:30.533 --> 00:04:33.168
So keep waiting off the common pool.

72
00:04:33.168 --> 00:04:42.536
On a pool of its own, sized for waiting, the 16 lookups wait together and finish in 419 milliseconds, and the tiny task waits 5.

73
00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:46.410
So, does a right total prove the work was shared?

74
00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:46.596
No.

75
00:04:46.596 --> 00:04:51.626
Cooks split their own piles, and idle cooks steal the big bundle from the bottom.

76
00:04:51.626 --> 00:04:55.539
Fork, compute, then join, and trust the referee, not the total.

77
00:04:55.539 --> 00:04:58.333
And keep the phone out of the shared kitchen.

78
00:04:59.033 --> 00:04:59.823
Quick check.

79
00:04:59.823 --> 00:05:03.310
Two million payments, and a threshold of one million.

80
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.219
How many cooks ever get work?

81
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:08.470
Two.

82
00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:14.421
One split makes two leaves of a million each, so the other two cooks never get a bundle.
